00:38:38.310 --> 00:38:42.330
OK, we're moving to what's now
in chapter 3.4,

00:38:42.330 --> 00:38:45.150
which this problem I'm going to
propose at first seems like it

00:38:45.150 --> 00:38:47.850
has nothing to do with what we
were just discussing, but as we

00:38:47.850 --> 00:38:49.010
will soon see,

00:38:49.010 --> 00:38:50.670
they're closely related.

00:38:50.670 --> 00:38:54.550
How I'll introduce this is by
calling it the approximation

00:38:54.550 --> 00:38:55.550
problem.

00:39:01.390 --> 00:39:03.830
And here's how I want you to
think about it.

00:39:03.830 --> 00:39:06.210
Pretend I have two vectors, u
and v.

00:39:06.210 --> 00:39:09.090
As usual, I've drawn them as
being in R2, but it could be in

00:39:09.090 --> 00:39:10.590
any universe,

00:39:10.770 --> 00:39:15.270
as long as they both have the
same number of components.

00:39:15.270 --> 00:39:18.050
Here u and v don't point in the
same direction.

00:39:18.050 --> 00:39:20.150
Here is the question, and this
is really what we're going to

00:39:20.150 --> 00:39:22.230
answer for the rest of today.

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:27.490
Among all vectors that point in
the same direction as v,

00:39:28.310 --> 00:39:31.550
that is among all scalar
multiples of v,

00:39:31.550 --> 00:39:36.050
which one is closest to u?

00:39:37.010 --> 00:39:40.410
Okay, what are some of the
scalar multiples of v?

00:39:40.410 --> 00:39:43.350
Well, this is a scalar multiple
of v,

00:39:43.350 --> 00:39:46.430
this is a scalar multiple of v,
this is a scalar multiple of v,

00:39:46.430 --> 00:39:48.930
This is a scalar multiple of v.
Those are all scalar multiples

00:39:48.930 --> 00:39:50.190
of v.

00:39:50.190 --> 00:39:51.590
So are these.

00:39:51.590 --> 00:39:54.930
I could look in the opposite way
if I needed to.

00:39:54.930 --> 00:39:57.330
Now, for all of these,

00:39:57.330 --> 00:40:01.110
the relative angle between u and
that scalar multiple is not

00:40:01.110 --> 00:40:02.110
changing.

00:40:02.150 --> 00:40:04.330
That angle only has two
possibilities.

00:40:04.330 --> 00:40:08.230
Either it's theta if I'm
multiplying v by a positive

00:40:08.230 --> 00:40:12.790
number, or it's 180 minus theta
if I'm multiplying v by a

00:40:12.790 --> 00:40:14.290
negative number.
Okay,

00:40:14.610 --> 00:40:17.770
so the question I'm asking here
is not one that is directly

00:40:17.770 --> 00:40:20.610
related to the angle.
I'm not asking which of the

00:40:20.610 --> 00:40:24.360
scalar multiples are closest in
terms of cosine similarity.

00:40:25.410 --> 00:40:28.410
Instead I'm asking something
that feels a little different.

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:30.450
Let me clearly write what I'm
saying.

00:40:30.450 --> 00:40:35.630
among all scalar

00:40:35.630 --> 00:40:38.570
multiples of v,

00:40:40.310 --> 00:40:45.530
which is closest to u.

00:40:50.710 --> 00:40:52.270
Now,

00:40:54.990 --> 00:40:59.550
we need to make the problem of
doing this, or problem of

00:40:59.550 --> 00:41:02.730
finding the scalar multiple that
is closest to you, a little more

00:41:02.730 --> 00:41:03.730
precise.

00:41:03.790 --> 00:41:09.040
So why don't we just define a
variable

00:41:09.990 --> 00:41:13.740
that corresponds to the amount
we are going to multiply v by?

00:41:19.130 --> 00:41:21.310
So let's call that variable k,

00:41:21.310 --> 00:41:26.560
and we'll use this for the rest
of the lecture.

00:41:33.130 --> 00:41:37.370
And so the real question is,

00:41:37.370 --> 00:41:42.150
which k makes kv,

00:41:43.070 --> 00:41:48.270
which is a scalar multiple of v
in which I multiplied v by k,

00:41:48.270 --> 00:41:52.770
as close to u as possible?

00:41:57.910 --> 00:41:58.910
Now,

00:41:59.350 --> 00:42:03.470
if we're not asking a question
of the direction, because you

00:42:03.470 --> 00:42:07.530
and Kv always live on the same
line, so we're not really asking

00:42:07.530 --> 00:42:10.850
which of those is more closely
aligned with you,

00:42:10.850 --> 00:42:15.350
how else could I measure how
close Kv is to you?

00:42:15.710 --> 00:42:17.750
If not with the angle between
them, what else could I do?

00:42:17.750 --> 00:42:19.010
Yeah, sorry.

00:42:19.010 --> 00:42:21.260
What's the perpendicular
distance between them?

00:42:22.550 --> 00:42:24.810
Well, we'll get to something
about perpendicularity in a

00:42:24.810 --> 00:42:25.470
second.

00:42:25.470 --> 00:42:28.070
But what if I'm just talking
about u and v? I think you have

00:42:28.070 --> 00:42:30.310
the right idea.
How do I measure how far apart u

00:42:30.310 --> 00:42:31.390
and v are?

00:42:31.390 --> 00:42:32.710
Sure, I could find the angle
between them.

00:42:32.710 --> 00:42:33.910
That's one way.
But what's another way I could

00:42:33.910 --> 00:42:36.670
find how far apart u and v are?

00:42:36.670 --> 00:42:36.950
Right.

00:42:36.950 --> 00:42:38.050
Any difference?

00:42:38.050 --> 00:42:43.130
Yeah, I could just find sort of
using

00:42:43.130 --> 00:42:45.690
the length of the vector between
them,

00:42:45.690 --> 00:42:48.510
u minus v.
I could subtract the two.

00:42:48.810 --> 00:42:53.410
That'll tell me how to go from v
to u, or if I do it the opposite

00:42:53.410 --> 00:42:54.650
way, from u to v.

00:42:54.650 --> 00:42:57.270
And I can see how long that
vector is.

00:42:57.270 --> 00:42:59.050
Do we agree?

00:42:59.050 --> 00:43:04.300
So the idea is, why do we
calculate the difference?

00:43:07.810 --> 00:43:12.690
This is the vector u minus v.
And remember, we talked about

00:43:12.690 --> 00:43:16.440
how you would determine that
this is the order before.

00:43:16.910 --> 00:43:20.550
v plus this vector cancels out
the v we already had and gives

00:43:20.550 --> 00:43:23.630
us a u, which is what the end
result is.

00:43:23.630 --> 00:43:26.390
And I shouldn't write u minus v.
I should really write u minus

00:43:26.390 --> 00:43:27.130
kv.

00:43:27.130 --> 00:43:31.170
And the question is, well, what
k makes this vector?

